Server-sent events (SSE) are used to stream content from certain operations. SSE is a web standard that allows a server to push data to a client over a single HTTP connection in real-time.

{{if isCsharpV2}}These operations return the event stream as an `IAsyncEnumerable<T>`. The stream can be enumerated only once; a second enumeration yields no events.{{else}}These operations return an instance of `EventStream`, which implements `IAsyncEnumerable<T>`, `IAsyncDisposable` and `IDisposable`. An `EventStream` can only be consumed once and cannot be replayed.{{end}}

Consume the stream with `await foreach`, which reads events as they arrive and disposes the stream when enumeration ends.{{if .Global.Config.EnableCancellationToken}}{{if isCsharpV2}} Cancelling the `CancellationToken` passed to the operation, or one passed with `WithCancellation(cancellationToken)`, ends the enumeration with an `OperationCanceledException`.{{else}} To cancel iteration, pass the same `CancellationToken` to the operation and to `eventStream.WithCancellation(cancellationToken)`.{{end}}{{end}}

{{templateUsageSection "eventstream"}}

{{if isCsharpV2}}The HTTP response stays open until enumeration ends: the stream completes, `break` exits the loop, the enumeration is cancelled or an exception is thrown. Enumerate every returned stream.{{else}}For finer-grained control, you may call `Next()` until it returns `null`. `Next()` never disposes the `EventStream`, so wrap it in `using`/`await using` or dispose it explicitly.{{end}}
